is  a  menu driven program which partitions disks using the stan‐
dard Apple disk partitioning scheme described in  "Inside  Macin‐
tosh:  Devices".   It does not support the Intel/DOS partitioning
scheme supported by Supported options are: Abbreviate the  parti‐
tion  types shown in the partition list.  Causes to always ignore
the device size listed in the partition table and compute the de‐
vice size by other means.  Turns on debugging.  Doesn’t add  that
much  output,  but does add a new command to the editing commands
that accesses an eclectic bunch  of  undocumented  functionality.
Show  HFS  volume names instead of partition name when available.
Prints a short help message.  Causes to go  into  an  interactive
mode  similar to the MacOS version of the program.  List the par‐
tition tables for the specified Show partition limits in  logical
blocks.   Default  is  physical blocks.  Prevents from writing to
the device.  Prints the version number of An  argument  which  is
simply the name of a indicates that should edit the partition ta‐
ble  of that device.  The current top level editing commands are:
C    (create with type also specified) c    create new  partition
d     delete a partition h    command help i    initialize parti‐
tion map n    (re)name a partition P    (print  ordered  by  base
address)  p    print the partition table q    quit editing (don’t
save changes) r    reorder partition entry  in  map  s     change
size  of partition map t    change the type of an existing parti‐
tion w    write the partition table Commands which take arguments
prompt for each argument in turn.  You can also type  any  number
of  the  arguments  separated by spaces and those prompts will be
skipped.  The only exception to typeahead  are  the  confirmation
prompts  on  the  and commands, since if we expect you to confirm
the decision, we shouldn’t undermine that by allowing you  to  be
precipitate  about  it.  Partitions are always specified by their
number, which is the index of the partition entry in  the  parti‐
tion  map.  Most of the commands will change the index numbers of
all partitions after the affected partition.  You are advised  to
print the table as frequently as necessary.  The (create new par‐
tition)  command is the only one with complicated arguments.  The
first argument is the base address (in blocks) of the  partition.
Besides  a  raw  number,  you can also specify a partition number
followed by the letter to indicate that the first  block  of  the
new  partition  should be the same as the first block of that ex‐
isting free space partition.  The second argument is  the  length
of the partition in blocks.  This can be a raw number or can be a
partition  number  followed by the letter to use the size of that
partition or can be a number followed by or to indicate the  size
in  kilobytes,  megabytes, or gigabytes respectively.  (These are
powers of 1024, of course, not powers of 1000.)  The third  argu‐
ment  is  the  name  of the partition.  This can be a single word
without quotes, or  a  string  surrounded  by  single  or  double
quotes.    The   type  of  the  created  partition  will  be  Ap‐
ple_UNIX_SVR2, which is the correct type for use with  This  com‐
mand  will  prompt  for  the  unix filesystem slice to set in the
Block Zero Block bits.  The command is similar  to  the  command,
with  the  addition  of a partition type argument after the other
arguments.  Choosing a type of Apple_UNIX_SVR2  will  prompt  for
the  unix  filesystem  slice to set in the Block Zero Block bits.
The (initalize) command prompts for the size of the device.   The
(name)  command  allows  the  name  of a partition to be changed.
Note that the various "Apple_Driver"  partitions  depend  on  the
name field for proper functioning.  We are not aware of any other
partition  types with this limitation.  The (reorder) command al‐
lows the index number of partitions to  be  changed.   The  index
numbers are constrained to be a contiguous sequence.  The (change
partition  type)  command  allows  the  type of a partition to be
changed.  Changing the type to Apple_UNIX_SVR2  will  prompt  for
the  unix  filesystem  slice to set in the Block Zero Block bits.
The (write) command writes the partition map out.   In  order  to
use the new partition map you must reboot.  The utility was orig‐
inally  developed  for MkLinux.  Some people believe there should
really be just one disk partitioning utility.  Filesystem  volume
names are out of place in a partition utility.  This utility sup‐
ports  HFS  volume  names,  but  not  volume  names  of any other
filesystem types.  The option has not been heavily tested.   will
